insert into public.concepts (slug, topic, difficulty, pro, title, data) values ('cors', 'security', 'beginner', false, 'CORS is the browser protecting your users', '{"slug":"cors","title":"CORS is the browser protecting your users","topic":"security","difficulty":"beginner","minutes":5,"pro":false,"related":["jwt-auth","caching-strategies","supabase-rls"],"dek":"That red console error isn''t your API being broken. It''s the browser refusing to let one website read another website''s responses — unless the server says it''s fine.","hook":"Every web developer has seen it: \"blocked by CORS policy\". The request works in Postman. It works with curl. It just doesn''t work from your frontend. The instinctive fix — `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` and move on — sometimes works and sometimes opens a hole. Knowing *who* is enforcing the rule, and *why*, makes the right fix obvious.","yap":"CORS isn''t a server firewall — it''s the browser asking the server \"may this website read your response?\". Postman works because Postman isn''t protecting anyone.","basics":"### The same-origin policy\n\nBrowsers enforce a rule: a web page can only **read** responses from its own **origin** — the combination of scheme, host and port.\n\n- `https://app.sumitsk.in` and `https://api.sumitsk.in` → different origins (different host)\n- `http://localhost:5173` and `http://localhost:3000` → different origins (different port)\n\nWhy? Imagine you''re logged into your bank in one tab, and visit a malicious site in another. Without this rule, the malicious page could call your bank''s API — your cookies would be sent automatically — and read your account details.\n\n### CORS relaxes the rule, safely\n\n**CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing)** lets a server say \"it''s fine for these other origins to read my responses\":\n\n```http\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.sumitsk.in\n```\n\nThe **browser** checks this header. If the page''s origin isn''t allowed, the browser blocks your JavaScript from seeing the response — even though the server may have already processed the request.\n\nThat''s why curl and Postman \"work\": they''re not browsers and don''t enforce CORS.","hood":"### Simple requests vs preflight\n\nFor \"simple\" requests — `GET`, `HEAD` or `POST` with form-style content types and no custom headers — the browser sends the request and checks the response headers.\n\nFor anything else — `PUT`, `DELETE`, `Content-Type: application/json`, an `Authorization` header — the browser first sends a **preflight** `OPTIONS` request:\n\n```http\nOPTIONS /api/answers\nOrigin: https://techyapper.sumitsk.in\nAccess-Control-Request-Method: POST\nAccess-Control-Request-Headers: content-type, authorization\n```\n\nThe server must reply with matching permissions:\n\n```http\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: https://techyapper.sumitsk.in\nAccess-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST\nAccess-Control-Allow-Headers: content-type, authorization\nAccess-Control-Max-Age: 600\n```\n\nOnly then does the browser send the real request. `Max-Age` lets it cache that permission.\n\n### Credentials\n\nTo send cookies cross-origin, the frontend uses `credentials: ''include''` and the server must respond with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` **and** a specific origin — the wildcard `*` is not allowed with credentials.","deep":"### What CORS is not\n\n- **Not authentication**: anyone can call your API from a server, script or curl. CORS only governs what browsers let *pages* read.\n- **Not CSRF protection by itself**: a cross-site form POST can still be *sent* and processed; CORS only stops the attacker''s page reading the response. Use SameSite cookies and CSRF tokens for state-changing endpoints.\n- **Not a server-side block**: for simple requests, the server receives and handles them regardless. Side effects still happen.\n\n### Dangerous configurations\n\n- **Reflecting the Origin header** back with `Allow-Credentials: true` for any origin effectively disables the same-origin policy for your users'' authenticated sessions.\n- **Sloppy matching**: allowing anything that *ends with* `sumitsk.in` also allows `evilsumitsk.in`. Match exact origins or proper subdomains.\n- **Trusting `null` origin**: sandboxed iframes and local files send `Origin: null`; allowing it can be exploited.\n\n### Caching gotcha\n\nIf your server varies `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` per request origin, it must send `Vary: Origin` — otherwise a CDN may cache a response allowed for one origin and serve it to another, causing confusing intermittent CORS failures.\n\n### Often the best fix is no CORS\n\nServe the frontend and API from the same origin (e.g. `/api` via a reverse proxy or your hosting platform''s rewrites) and CORS never comes up. Dev servers do the same with a proxy config.","myths":[{"myth":"CORS protects my API from unauthorised access","truth":"It controls what browsers let pages read. Servers, scripts and tools can call your API freely."},{"myth":"The CORS error means the request never reached the server","truth":"Simple requests are sent and processed; the browser just hides the response. Preflighted requests are stopped before the real request."},{"myth":"`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` works for everything","truth":"It''s not allowed when credentials (cookies) are included."}],"facts":["The same-origin policy dates back to Netscape Navigator 2.0 in 1995, introduced alongside JavaScript.","CORS is now defined in the WHATWG Fetch standard rather than its own specification."],"realWorld":["Configuring allowed origins for a frontend on one subdomain calling an API on another.","Supabase and Firebase APIs sending permissive CORS headers because their security relies on tokens and RLS, not origin.","Using Vite''s dev-server proxy to avoid CORS during local development."],"check":{"prompt":"Your API works from curl, but the browser shows \"blocked by CORS policy\" for a `fetch` with `Content-Type: application/json`. What''s the most likely missing piece?","options":["The API needs HTTPS","The server doesn''t answer the OPTIONS preflight with the right `Access-Control-Allow-*` headers","curl is caching the response","JSON can''t be sent cross-origin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"JSON content-type triggers a preflight. If the server doesn''t respond to `OPTIONS` with an allowed origin, methods and headers, the browser never sends the real request. curl doesn''t do preflights at all."}}'::jsonb)
