insert into public.concepts (slug, topic, difficulty, pro, title, data) values ('embeddings-vector-db', 'ai', 'intermediate', true, 'Embeddings and vector databases', '{"slug":"embeddings-vector-db","title":"Embeddings and vector databases","topic":"ai","difficulty":"intermediate","minutes":6,"pro":true,"related":["rag","llm-tokens","sql-indexes"],"dek":"Turn meaning into coordinates, then search by distance. The quiet idea behind semantic search, recommendations and almost every RAG system.","hook":"Search \"my card got declined\" in a classic keyword engine and you miss the help article titled \"Payment failures\". Same meaning, zero shared words. Embeddings fix this by turning text into points in space where *meaning* is distance. Vector databases are what you get when you need to search millions of those points in milliseconds.","yap":"An embedding turns text into a list of numbers where similar meanings land close together. A vector database is just a very fast way to find the nearest neighbours.","basics":"An **embedding** is a list of numbers (a **vector**) that represents the meaning of a piece of text — or an image, or audio. Typical embedding models produce vectors with hundreds to a few thousand numbers.\n\nThe magic property: **similar meanings produce nearby vectors**.\n\n```text\n\"reset my password\"      → [0.12, -0.48, 0.33, …]\n\"I can''t log in\"          → [0.10, -0.45, 0.31, …]   ← close\n\"best biryani in Hyderabad\" → [-0.71, 0.22, 0.05, …]  ← far away\n```\n\nTo search:\n\n1. Embed all your documents once and store the vectors.\n2. When a query arrives, embed it with the **same model**.\n3. Find the stored vectors closest to the query vector.\n\nThat''s **semantic search**. A **vector database** (or a vector extension like pgvector for Postgres) stores the vectors and finds nearest neighbours quickly.\n\n\"Closeness\" is usually measured with **cosine similarity** — how much two vectors point in the same direction.","hood":"### Where the numbers come from\n\nEmbedding models are neural networks (often transformer encoders) trained so that related pairs — a question and its answer, a caption and its image — end up close and unrelated pairs end up far apart (**contrastive learning**). The individual dimensions aren''t human-readable; meaning lives in the geometry.\n\n### Distance metrics\n\n- **Cosine similarity**: angle between vectors; ignores length. The common default for text.\n- **Dot product**: equals cosine when vectors are normalised to length 1 — many models output normalised vectors for this reason.\n- **Euclidean (L2) distance**: straight-line distance.\n\nUse the metric the model was trained for.\n\n### Why you can''t just scan\n\nComparing a query against 10 million 1,536-dimensional vectors is billions of multiplications per search. Exact search doesn''t scale, so vector databases use **approximate nearest neighbour (ANN)** indexes, trading a little accuracy (recall) for huge speed-ups.\n\n### Postgres can do this\n\n```sql\ncreate extension vector;\ncreate table docs (id bigint primary key, body text, embedding vector(1536));\ncreate index on docs using hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);\n\nselect id, body from docs\norder by embedding <=> $1   -- cosine distance to the query vector\nlimit 5;\n```\n\nFor many apps, pgvector inside an existing Postgres (including Supabase) beats running a separate vector database.","deep":"### ANN index families\n\n- **HNSW** (Hierarchical Navigable Small World): a multi-layer graph; search hops from coarse to fine layers. Excellent recall and speed, higher memory use and slower builds. Tunables: `m` (connections per node), `ef_construction`, `ef_search`.\n- **IVF** (inverted file): cluster vectors into lists with k-means; search only the nearest few lists (`probes`). Faster to build, needs training data, recall depends on probes.\n- **Product quantisation** and other compression: store vectors in fewer bytes to fit billions in memory, at some precision cost.\n\n### Filtering is the hard part\n\n\"Nearest neighbours **where tenant_id = 42 and published = true**\" is surprisingly tricky. Post-filtering after ANN search can return too few results; pre-filtering can defeat the index. Approaches include filtered HNSW variants, partitioning per tenant, and iterative scans (pgvector added iterative index scans in 0.8 for exactly this).\n\n### Hybrid search\n\nEmbeddings are weak at exact identifiers: error codes, SKUs, names. Production systems usually combine **BM25 keyword search** with vector search and merge results (for example with Reciprocal Rank Fusion), often followed by a **re-ranker** model that scores the top candidates more precisely.\n\n### Practical gotchas\n\n- **Never mix models**: vectors from different embedding models (or versions) aren''t comparable. Changing models means re-embedding everything.\n- **Chunk size matters**: embedding a 30-page PDF as one vector blurs everything together; chunks of a few hundred tokens with context work better.\n- **Dimensions cost money**: storage and memory scale with dimension × rows. Some models support shortened (\"Matryoshka\") embeddings with small quality loss.","myths":[{"myth":"Vector databases understand meaning","truth":"The embedding model encodes meaning. The database only does fast geometry."},{"myth":"You need a dedicated vector database","truth":"For millions of vectors, pgvector in Postgres is often enough and keeps data in one place."},{"myth":"Semantic search replaces keyword search","truth":"It complements it. Exact terms like codes and names still need keyword matching."}],"facts":["Word2Vec (2013) popularised the famous example where vector(\"king\") − vector(\"man\") + vector(\"woman\") lands near vector(\"queen\").","HNSW was described by Yury Malkov and Dmitry Yashunin in 2016 and is now the most widely used ANN index."],"realWorld":["Semantic search over docs and support tickets.","\"Similar items\" recommendations in e-commerce and content apps.","The retrieval step of RAG chatbots.","De-duplicating near-identical records and clustering feedback by theme."],"check":{"prompt":"You switch to a newer, better embedding model for new documents but keep the old vectors for existing ones. What happens to search quality?","options":["It improves across the board","It breaks — vectors from different models live in different spaces and can''t be meaningfully compared","Nothing changes, because cosine similarity normalises differences","Only the number of dimensions needs to match"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each model learns its own geometry. Comparing a query from model B against documents from model A is like comparing coordinates from two different maps. Re-embed everything when you switch."}}'::jsonb)
