insert into public.concepts (slug, topic, difficulty, pro, title, data) values ('js-event-loop', 'languages', 'intermediate', false, 'The JavaScript event loop', '{"slug":"js-event-loop","title":"The JavaScript event loop","topic":"languages","difficulty":"intermediate","minutes":6,"pro":false,"related":["nodejs-event-loop","react-useeffect","python-gil"],"dek":"One thread, one call stack, and a very disciplined queue. How JavaScript does \"many things at once\" without doing two things at once.","hook":"JavaScript runs your code on a single thread. Yet a browser tab can animate, fetch, listen for clicks and run timers all at the same time. The trick isn''t parallelism — it''s a loop with strict rules about what goes next. Once you know the rules, puzzles like \"why did my `setTimeout(0)` run late?\" stop being puzzles.","yap":"JavaScript isn''t async because it''s multi-threaded. It''s async because it never waits — it hands slow work to the runtime and picks up the callback when the stack is empty.","basics":"Think of a single chef in a kitchen. The chef can only chop one thing at a time — that''s the **call stack**. But the chef doesn''t stand watching water boil. They set a timer, start something else, and come back when it rings.\n\nIn JavaScript:\n\n- The **call stack** runs your code, one function at a time.\n- Slow things — timers, network requests, file reads — are handed to the **runtime** (the browser or Node), which does them outside your thread.\n- When they finish, their callbacks wait in a **queue**.\n- The **event loop** keeps checking: *is the stack empty?* If yes, it takes the next callback from the queue and runs it.\n\n```js\nconsole.log(''order placed'')\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(''food ready''), 2000)\nconsole.log(''taking next order'')\n```\n\nThis logs \"order placed\", \"taking next order\", then two seconds later \"food ready\". The timer didn''t block anything.\n\nThe flip side: if your own code takes 3 seconds to run (a giant loop), *nothing* else happens for those 3 seconds. No clicks, no rendering. The chef is busy.","hood":"### Two queues, not one\n\nThere are actually (at least) two kinds of queued work:\n\n- **Macrotasks** (often just called tasks): `setTimeout`, `setInterval`, I/O callbacks, UI events like clicks.\n- **Microtasks**: promise callbacks (`.then`, `await` continuations) and `queueMicrotask`.\n\nThe rule: after each macrotask finishes, the engine **drains the entire microtask queue** before doing anything else — including rendering.\n\n```js\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(''timeout''), 0)\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(''promise''))\nconsole.log(''sync'')\n// sync → promise → timeout\n```\n\n### Where rendering fits\n\nIn browsers, between macrotasks the event loop may run a **rendering step**: `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks, style, layout, paint — roughly every 16ms on a 60Hz screen. A long task delays that step, which is what users perceive as jank. Chrome flags any task over 50ms as a \"long task\".\n\n### `await` is sugar over microtasks\n\n```js\nasync function save() {\n  console.log(1)\n  await db.write()\n  console.log(2) // runs later, as a microtask\n}\n```\n\nEverything after `await` is scheduled as a continuation. The function returns a promise immediately; the stack unwinds; other work proceeds.\n\n### setTimeout(fn, 0) isn''t zero\n\nIt means \"queue this as a macrotask, at the earliest after 0ms\". It still waits for the current stack and all microtasks. Browsers also clamp nested timers to at least 4ms after a few levels.","deep":"### Starving the loop with microtasks\n\nBecause microtasks are drained completely, a microtask that queues another microtask forever will freeze the page just as effectively as a `while(true)` — rendering never gets a turn:\n\n```js\nfunction forever() { Promise.resolve().then(forever) }\n```\n\n### Breaking up long work\n\nFor CPU-heavy work on the main thread you have options, roughly in order of preference:\n\n1. **Move it off the main thread** with a Web Worker (true parallelism, message passing, no DOM access).\n2. **Yield** periodically so input and rendering get a turn: `await scheduler.yield()` where supported, or `await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r))`.\n3. **Schedule by priority** with `scheduler.postTask` (user-blocking, user-visible, background).\n\nReact''s concurrent rendering is built on exactly this idea: it renders in small slices and yields back to the browser, so typing stays responsive while a big list renders.\n\n### Interaction to Next Paint\n\nGoogle''s Core Web Vital **INP** measures how long it takes from user input until the next frame is painted. Long tasks, heavy event handlers and big synchronous re-renders are the usual culprits. Event-loop literacy is now a performance metric, not trivia.\n\n### The spec reality\n\nThe HTML spec defines multiple task sources (timers, networking, user interaction, …) and lets browsers prioritise between them. So \"macrotasks run in FIFO order\" is a useful model, not a guarantee across sources. Microtask ordering, however, is strictly specified.","myths":[{"myth":"`setTimeout(fn, 0)` runs immediately","truth":"It runs after the current code and all pending microtasks — and possibly after rendering."},{"myth":"`async` functions run on another thread","truth":"They run on the same thread; `await` just splits the function into continuations scheduled as microtasks."},{"myth":"Promises make CPU-heavy code non-blocking","truth":"Wrapping a heavy loop in a promise still runs it on the main thread. Only workers give you another thread."}],"facts":["The term \"event loop\" predates JavaScript — GUI toolkits and network servers used the pattern long before browsers did.","Philip Roberts'' 2014 talk \"What the heck is the event loop anyway?\" remains one of the most-watched JS conference talks ever."],"realWorld":["Debugging a frozen UI: Chrome DevTools'' Performance panel shows long tasks as red-flagged blocks.","Animation libraries rely on `requestAnimationFrame` to sync with the rendering step.","Interview classic: \"predict the log order\" questions mixing promises and timers."],"check":{"prompt":"What is logged?","code":{"lang":"js","source":"setTimeout(() => console.log(''A''), 0)\nqueueMicrotask(() => console.log(''B''))\nconsole.log(''C'')"},"options":["A B C","C A B","C B A","B C A"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Synchronous `C` first. Then microtasks drain: `B`. Then the next macrotask, the timer: `A`."}}'::jsonb)
